{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016g1xgMWJLAHCar6h1iuoDx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Scout — 2026-10-06\n2\t\n3\tDay 17. Sixteen templates are on the shelf, and most of the recent ones are\n4\tone person's PB card made from their hobby's file: speedrun, powerlifting,\n5\tswim, sim racing, cubing. So today I looked for a community picture made\n6\tweekly by a volunteer for a whole group, and for constructs no earlier scout\n7\topened. Eight parallel sweeps covered:\n8\t\n9\t- monthly art challenges (Inktober runs now)\n10\t- novel word-count challenges\n11\t- Concept2 rowing\n12\t- peak-bagging lists\n13\t- roguelike and rhythm-game runs\n14\t- jam-band setlists\n15\t- CrossFit and parkrun\n16\t- a wildcard pass (bell ringing, coffee roasting)\n17\t\n18\tEach sweep's notes are in [`scout-notes/`](scout-notes/).\n19\t\n20\t## Candidates (3–5, best first)\n21\t\n22\t### 1. parkrun weekly event-summary card\n23\t\n24\t- Who: parkrun event teams. Each Saturday 5k event has a run director (RD)\n25\t  and a volunteer who writes the weekly run report, and they post the\n26\t  week's numbers to the event's Facebook page and its results/news page.\n27\t  There are events in 20+ countries: the Eventuate userscript lists\n28\t  parkrun.org.uk, .com.au, .us, .co.za, .ie, .ca, .jp and more under\n29\t  \"Applies to\" ([greasyfork](https://greasyfork.org/scripts/534157-eventuate)).\n30\t- The construct: **the weekly event and its results page**. Every run has\n31\t  an event name and run number (`#568`) and a date. It also has the same\n32\t  set of counts:\n33\t  - parkrunners (finishers)\n34\t  - new PBs\n35\t  - first-time parkrunners\n36\t  - visitors\n37\t  - volunteers and first-time volunteers\n38\t  - milestone clubs: 25 / 50 / 100 / 250 / 500 runs or volunteer credits\n39\t\n40\t  Volunteers have built three tools that read that page and write the\n41\t  counts down, which gives the field names. The clearest is\n42\t  [parkrun-runstats](https://github.com/rwkura/parkrun-milestones):\n43\t  `parkrunners: 568`, `New PBs: 78`, `Visitors: 65`,\n44\t  `First-time parkrunners: 45`, `Volunteers: 24`,\n45\t  `First-time volunteers: 6`, `Milestones: 4xR25, 4xR50, 1xR100`.\n46\t- The recurring need: once a week, per event, a picture of \"this week at\n47\t  our parkrun\" made from those numbers. Today it is made by hand in\n48\t  PowerPoint, or posted as text.\n49\t- Evidence:\n50\t  - [ear1grey/parkrun-event-summary](https://github.com/ear1grey/parkrun-event-summary)\n51\t    is the strongest by-hand quote of the day. The author \"saw a really cool\n52\t    infographic at my local parkrun, that was generated using a PowerPoint\n53\t    slide ... (for Rushmoor parkrun) that may in turn have been based on a\n54\t    design by Ian Gregory (an RD at Great Salterns) - there are many examples\n55\t    on the Rushmoor and Great Salterns Parkrun results pages. The process of\n56\t    getting the data into Powerpoint is quite cumbersome\". Their answer is a\n57\t    beta Chrome extension that draws the infographic inside the results\n58\t    page (3 stars, released Jan 2024).\n59\t  - [johnsyweb/eventuate](https://github.com/johnsyweb/eventuate) was\n60\t    written \"while volunteering as a Run Director at Brimbank parkrun, to\n61\t    celebrate our community on the Facebook page\". It extracts \"milestones,\n62\t    first-timers, personal bests, volunteers, and community facts\" to help\n63\t    \"write a weekly parkrun event report\". The output is text only. Version\n64\t    1.20.0 was [updated 2026-09-30](https://greasyfork.org/scripts/534157-eventuate),\n65\t    so it is maintained right now.\n66\t  - [parkrun-runstats](https://github.com/rwkura/parkrun-milestones)\n67\t    \"prints the stats of the latest run in list format; suitable for sharing\n68\t    in text-based social media\". The same repo predicts the next run's\n69\t    milestone candidates (25, 50, 100, 250, 500).\n70\t- Why m0saic fits: the picture is a fixed set of stat tiles. A header band\n71\t  holds the event name, `#run` and date. Under it sit six tiles (finishers,\n72\t  PBs, first timers, visitors, volunteers, first-time volunteers) and a\n73\t  milestone row with one badge per club and a count. The props would mirror\n74\t  the runstats fields, so the RD pastes numbers they already have. The\n75\t  render is aggregate-only, with no names. It is one render per event per\n76\t  Saturday, with feed, story and square from the same props, and batchable\n77\t  across a season or across the events in a region. Every count is a number\n78\t  with a default, so the defaults render a full card. It needs no media, no\n79\t  map and no art. It is also a group picture made weekly by a volunteer,\n80\t  which is a different job from the personal PB cards already on the shelf.\n81\t- Risks:\n82\t  - Three tools already pull these numbers, and one already draws an\n83\t    infographic. That extension only works in a browser, on parkrun's own\n84\t    page, in its own look, and makes no file you can post or batch. The\n85\t    critic should ask whether a template beats it.\n86\t  - parkrun is hostile to scraping, so the template must take numbers as\n87\t    props and never fetch.\n88\t  - \"parkrun\" is a trademark: no logo, no lookalike brand palette, a\n89\t    descriptive name.\n90\t  - Milestone rows vary (0 to 6 clubs in a week) and need a layout rule for\n91\t    an empty week.\n92\t  - I could not open a Rushmoor or Great Salterns page to show one of the\n93\t    PowerPoint originals, so their look is described, not seen.\n94\t\n95\t### 2. Bell ringing: first peal / first quarter peal card from a BellBoard record\n96\t\n97\t- Who: change ringers in UK (and Commonwealth) church towers. Their\n98\t  performances are recorded on BellBoard, The Ringing World's site, and\n99\t  guilds and the Central Council (CCCBR) run the community around them.\n100\t- The construct: the **peal or quarter peal record**. A\n101\t  [BellBoard record](https://bb.ringingworld.co.uk/view.php?id=1401162) has:\n102\t  - place and county, dedication, date\n103\t  - duration (\"in 3h 16\") and tenor weight\n104\t  - changes and method (\"5088 Plain Bob Major\") and composer\n105\t  - the ringers numbered 1..N by bell, with \"(C)\" for the conductor\n106\t  - footnotes\n107\t- The recurring need: a ringer's first quarter peal or first peal is a\n108\t  milestone, and the\n109\t  [CCCBR publicity page](https://archive.cccbr.org.uk/?p=4271) offers\n110\t  \"Congratulatory certificates: For a First peal ... For a First peal as\n111\t  conductor ... For a First quarter peal ... in either PDF or docx format\".\n112\t  Those are blank forms that someone fills in by hand, per ringer.\n113\t- Why m0saic fits: it is a fixed form. A header band holds tower, dedication\n114\t  and date. A centre block holds changes, method, time and tenor. A grid of\n115\t  ringers 1..N has one row per bell (6-12 rows) and a highlighted row for the\n116\t  honoree. A footnote band closes it. The data comes from one record, and a\n117\t  guild newsletter could batch every first quarter peal.\n118\t- Risks: it is a certificate, which is print, not a feed post. BellBoard's\n119\t  export returned HTTP 500 to us, so the XML field names are unverified.\n120\t  Tenor weight uses an en dash (write it as 20-0-26). I found no \"is there\n121\t  a tool\" quote. And the prestige object is the hand-painted peal board.\n122\t\n123\t### 3. Jam-band show setlist card from Songfish JSON\n124\t\n125\t- Who: fans of Goose, Phish and the bands on the Songfish platform\n126\t  (elgoose.net, Bearly Dead).\n127\t- The construct: the setlist, with sets, encore, `>` segues, jamchart marks,\n128\t  and debut and bustout gaps. The\n129\t  [elgoose API](https://elgoose.net/api/v2/latest.json) is keyless JSON with\n130\t  real fields: `showdate, venuename, setnumber, set_label, position,\n131\t  songname, transition, isjamchart, footnote, shownotes`. A shownote reads,\n132\t  for example, \"Franklin's Tower was played for the first time since\n133\t  December 14, 2024 (129 shows)\".\n134\t- The recurring need: a setlist picture after every show. A\n135\t  [2011 blogger](https://tomorrowsverse.com/story/introducing-the-visual-setlist-2798.html)\n136\t  hand-made \"Visual Setlists\" for Phish \"the morning after each\n137\t  performance\", colour-coding the gap since a song was last played.\n138\t- Why m0saic fits: set blocks stacked as rows, with segue arrows and\n139\t  coloured gap badges. The data comes from one keyless JSON call.\n140\t- Risks: the demand evidence is a 2011 blog and an app review. Bands post\n141\t  their own setlist graphics. A 20+ song show with long titles is the\n142\t  text-overflow defect again.\n143\t\n144\t### 4. Peak-list progress board (NH 48, ADK 46, Colorado 14ers)\n145\t\n146\t- Who: peak baggers working through a fixed list. The AMC\n147\t  [4000 Footer Club](https://amc4000footer.org/how-to-apply.html) asks\n148\t  climbers to \"note the dates of your ascents as you make them\" for an\n149\t  application in PDF or Excel.\n150\t- The construct: a list of N peaks, each with a date. The picture is N cells\n151\t  with filled or empty state, plus \"31/48\" and a finisher card at N/N.\n152\t- Evidence: a [VFTT thread](https://vftt.org/threads/peakbagger-spreadsheet.44642/post-373258)\n153\t  of hand-made Excel trackers (\"I want to post some of my stats to my\n154\t  blog\"), from 2012. Tracker apps show progress in-app only.\n155\t- Why m0saic fits: a 48-cell grid is pure rectangles with one state per\n156\t  cell.\n157\t- Risks: no one asks for a shareable image. Each person finishes a list\n158\t  once. Munro and Wainwright names are non-ASCII and the lists run to\n159\t  214-282 cells.\n160\t\n161\t### 5. Monthly art-challenge compilation grid (Inktober, Huevember)\n162\t\n163\t- Who: artists doing 31-day prompt challenges. Inktober is running now: the\n164\t  [official 2026 list](https://www.svslearn.com/news/2026/9/1/the-official-inktober-2026-prompt-list)\n165\t  says \"31 drawings in 31 days\".\n166\t- The construct: the prompt list (31 numbered words) plus the artist's daily\n167\t  drawings. Huevember's founder\n168\t  [wants the month compiled](https://xquissive.com/huevember): \"when you put\n169\t  all your artworks next to each other in order, you'd notice a sense of\n170\t  continuity through the colours\".\n171\t- Why m0saic fits: a 31-cell grid of image, day number and prompt word.\n172\t  With no images it renders the host's prompt-list graphic.\n173\t- Risks: there is no data file, and the audience lives in Procreate and\n174\t  Canva. Demand is implied, not shown. Collage apps cover most of it. The\n175\t  defaults would be empty placeholders.\n176\t\n177\t## Pick\n178\t\n179\t**parkrun weekly event-summary card.** It is the only candidate where an\n180\topened page says a volunteer team makes this exact picture by hand and that\n181\tdoing so is \"quite cumbersome\". The numbers are a fixed set that three\n182\tvolunteer tools already extract every week, which gives the props and their\n183\tdefaults. It also adds a weekly group picture made by event volunteers to a\n184\tshelf of personal PB cards. Bell ringing has the more charming construct,\n185\tbut its evidence is a blank docx certificate, not a recurring post.\n186\t\n187\t## Rejected today\n188\t\n189\t- NetHack ascension card (xlogfile `conduct`/`achieve` bitfields): a lovely\n190\t  rectangle shape, but ascension posts (\"YAAP\") are text plus dumplog links,\n191\t  and nobody I read makes a picture.\n192\t- Concept2 erg piece card: good numbers (split /500m, spm, watts, drag\n193\t  factor), but no opened page asks for a graphic, and ErgData shares a link.\n194\t- Word-count recap card (post-NaNoWriMo): Pacemaker, TrackBear and NaNo 2.0\n195\t  draw the par-line graph, and writers share screenshots of it.\n196\t- CrossFit daily WOD graphic: the workout is a free-text blob, and SugarWOD\n197\t  BoxTV and Canva packs serve it. A PR card had no evidence.\n198\t- parkrun personal milestone card (50/100/250): parkrun already recognises\n199\t  milestones with shirts, it needs names (non-ASCII and privacy risk), and\n200\t  I found no by-hand evidence.\n201\t- osu! replay thumbnails: [osr2png](https://github.com/xjunko/osr2png) is a\n202\t  CLI that already does it.\n203\t- Slay the Spire / Balatro run cards: run trackers serve them, and they need\n204\t  card art.\n205\t- Home coffee roasting (Artisan `.alog`): Artisan prints its own roast\n206\t  report.\n207\t\n208\t## Research record\n209\t\n210\t- Eight parallel sweeps ran about 55 web searches and opened about 35 pages.\n211\t  The per-angle notes, the searches and the failed URLs are in\n212\t  [`scout-notes/`](scout-notes/).\n213\t- I re-opened the pick's sources myself:\n214\t  - ear1grey/parkrun-event-summary\n215\t  - johnsyweb/eventuate\n216\t  - the Eventuate greasyfork page\n217\t  - rwkura/parkrun-milestones\n218\t  - the CCCBR publicity page\n219\t- Failed to open: the Walkhighlands forum and pixiv (403), BellBoard\n220\t  `export.php` (500), metacpan NAOdash (402) and TrackBear's help pages (403).\n221\t  Reddit was not tried (blocked since 2026-09-25).\n222\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aa0f356-0a54-4ead-ae70-b6948b14086a","uuid":"13f1593e-ef6d-45b1-8a1e-763aef191342","timestamp":"2026-10-06T16:10:26.437Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\journal\\2026-10-06\\10-scout.md","content":"# Scout — 2026-10-06\n\nDay 17. Sixteen templates are on the shelf, and most of the recent ones are\none person's PB card made from their hobby's file: speedrun, powerlifting,\nswim, sim racing, cubing. So today I looked for a community picture made\nweekly by a volunteer for a whole group, and for constructs no earlier scout\nopened. Eight parallel sweeps covered:\n\n- monthly art challenges (Inktober runs now)\n- novel word-count challenges\n- Concept2 rowing\n- peak-bagging lists\n- roguelike and rhythm-game runs\n- jam-band setlists\n- CrossFit and parkrun\n- a wildcard pass (bell ringing, coffee roasting)\n\nEach sweep's notes are in [`scout-notes/`](scout-notes/).\n\n## Candidates (3–5, best first)\n\n### 1. parkrun weekly event-summary card\n\n- Who: parkrun event teams. Each Saturday 5k event has a run director (RD)\n  and a volunteer who writes the weekly run report, and they post the\n  week's numbers to the event's Facebook page and its results/news page.\n  There are events in 20+ countries: the Eventuate userscript lists\n  parkrun.org.uk, .com.au, .us, .co.za, .ie, .ca, .jp and more under\n  \"Applies to\" ([greasyfork](https://greasyfork.org/scripts/534157-eventuate)).\n- The construct: **the weekly event and its results page**. Every run has\n  an event name and run number (`#568`) and a date. It also has the same\n  set of counts:\n  - parkrunners (finishers)\n  - new PBs\n  - first-time parkrunners\n  - visitors\n  - volunteers and first-time volunteers\n  - milestone clubs: 25 / 50 / 100 / 250 / 500 runs or volunteer credits\n\n  Volunteers have built three tools that read that page and write the\n  counts down, which gives the field names. The clearest is\n  [parkrun-runstats](https://github.com/rwkura/parkrun-milestones):\n  `parkrunners: 568`, `New PBs: 78`, `Visitors: 65`,\n  `First-time parkrunners: 45`, `Volunteers: 24`,\n  `First-time volunteers: 6`, `Milestones: 4xR25, 4xR50, 1xR100`.\n- The recurring need: once a week, per event, a picture of \"this week at\n  our parkrun\" made from those numbers. Today it is made by hand in\n  PowerPoint, or posted as text.\n- Evidence:\n  - [ear1grey/parkrun-event-summary](https://github.com/ear1grey/parkrun-event-summary)\n    is the strongest by-hand quote of the day. The author \"saw a really cool\n    infographic at my local parkrun, that was generated using a PowerPoint\n    slide ... (for Rushmoor parkrun) that may in turn have been based on a\n    design by Ian Gregory (an RD at Great Salterns) - there are many examples\n    on the Rushmoor and Great Salterns Parkrun results pages. The process of\n    getting the data into Powerpoint is quite cumbersome\". Their answer is a\n    beta Chrome extension that draws the infographic inside the results\n    page (3 stars, released Jan 2024).\n  - [johnsyweb/eventuate](https://github.com/johnsyweb/eventuate) was\n    written \"while volunteering as a Run Director at Brimbank parkrun, to\n    celebrate our community on the Facebook page\". It extracts \"milestones,\n    first-timers, personal bests, volunteers, and community facts\" to help\n    \"write a weekly parkrun event report\". The output is text only. Version\n    1.20.0 was [updated 2026-09-30](https://greasyfork.org/scripts/534157-eventuate),\n    so it is maintained right now.\n  - [parkrun-runstats](https://github.com/rwkura/parkrun-milestones)\n    \"prints the stats of the latest run in list format; suitable for sharing\n    in text-based social media\". The same repo predicts the next run's\n    milestone candidates (25, 50, 100, 250, 500).\n- Why m0saic fits: the picture is a fixed set of stat tiles. A header band\n  holds the event name, `#run` and date. Under it sit six tiles (finishers,\n  PBs, first timers, visitors, volunteers, first-time volunteers) and a\n  milestone row with one badge per club and a count. The props would mirror\n  the runstats fields, so the RD pastes numbers they already have. The\n  render is aggregate-only, with no names. It is one render per event per\n  Saturday, with feed, story and square from the same props, and batchable\n  across a season or across the events in a region. Every count is a number\n  with a default, so the defaults render a full card. It needs no media, no\n  map and no art. It is also a group picture made weekly by a volunteer,\n  which is a different job from the personal PB cards already on the shelf.\n- Risks:\n  - Three tools already pull these numbers, and one already draws an\n    infographic. That extension only works in a browser, on parkrun's own\n    page, in its own look, and makes no file you can post or batch. The\n    critic should ask whether a template beats it.\n  - parkrun is hostile to scraping, so the template must take numbers as\n    props and never fetch.\n  - \"parkrun\" is a trademark: no logo, no lookalike brand palette, a\n    descriptive name.\n  - Milestone rows vary (0 to 6 clubs in a week) and need a layout rule for\n    an empty week.\n  - I could not open a Rushmoor or Great Salterns page to show one of the\n    PowerPoint originals, so their look is described, not seen.\n\n### 2. Bell ringing: first peal / first quarter peal card from a BellBoard record\n\n- Who: change ringers in UK (and Commonwealth) church towers. Their\n  performances are recorded on BellBoard, The Ringing World's site, and\n  guilds and the Central Council (CCCBR) run the community around them.\n- The construct: the **peal or quarter peal record**. A\n  [BellBoard record](https://bb.ringingworld.co.uk/view.php?id=1401162) has:\n  - place and county, dedication, date\n  - duration (\"in 3h 16\") and tenor weight\n  - changes and method (\"5088 Plain Bob Major\") and composer\n  - the ringers numbered 1..N by bell, with \"(C)\" for the conductor\n  - footnotes\n- The recurring need: a ringer's first quarter peal or first peal is a\n  milestone, and the\n  [CCCBR publicity page](https://archive.cccbr.org.uk/?p=4271) offers\n  \"Congratulatory certificates: For a First peal ... For a First peal as\n  conductor ... For a First quarter peal ... in either PDF or docx format\".\n  Those are blank forms that someone fills in by hand, per ringer.\n- Why m0saic fits: it is a fixed form. A header band holds tower, dedication\n  and date. A centre block holds changes, method, time and tenor. A grid of\n  ringers 1..N has one row per bell (6-12 rows) and a highlighted row for the\n  honoree. A footnote band closes it. The data comes from one record, and a\n  guild newsletter could batch every first quarter peal.\n- Risks: it is a certificate, which is print, not a feed post. BellBoard's\n  export returned HTTP 500 to us, so the XML field names are unverified.\n  Tenor weight uses an en dash (write it as 20-0-26). I found no \"is there\n  a tool\" quote. And the prestige object is the hand-painted peal board.\n\n### 3. Jam-band show setlist card from Songfish JSON\n\n- Who: fans of Goose, Phish and the bands on the Songfish platform\n  (elgoose.net, Bearly Dead).\n- The construct: the setlist, with sets, encore, `>` segues, jamchart marks,\n  and debut and bustout gaps. The\n  [elgoose API](https://elgoose.net/api/v2/latest.json) is keyless JSON with\n  real fields: `showdate, venuename, setnumber, set_label, position,\n  songname, transition, isjamchart, footnote, shownotes`. A shownote reads,\n  for example, \"Franklin's Tower was played for the first time since\n  December 14, 2024 (129 shows)\".\n- The recurring need: a setlist picture after every show. A\n  [2011 blogger](https://tomorrowsverse.com/story/introducing-the-visual-setlist-2798.html)\n  hand-made \"Visual Setlists\" for Phish \"the morning after each\n  performance\", colour-coding the gap since a song was last played.\n- Why m0saic fits: set blocks stacked as rows, with segue arrows and\n  coloured gap badges. The data comes from one keyless JSON call.\n- Risks: the demand evidence is a 2011 blog and an app review. Bands post\n  their own setlist graphics. A 20+ song show with long titles is the\n  text-overflow defect again.\n\n### 4. Peak-list progress board (NH 48, ADK 46, Colorado 14ers)\n\n- Who: peak baggers working through a fixed list. The AMC\n  [4000 Footer Club](https://amc4000footer.org/how-to-apply.html) asks\n  climbers to \"note the dates of your ascents as you make them\" for an\n  application in PDF or Excel.\n- The construct: a list of N peaks, each with a date. The picture is N cells\n  with filled or empty state, plus \"31/48\" and a finisher card at N/N.\n- Evidence: a [VFTT thread](https://vftt.org/threads/peakbagger-spreadsheet.44642/post-373258)\n  of hand-made Excel trackers (\"I want to post some of my stats to my\n  blog\"), from 2012. Tracker apps show progress in-app only.\n- Why m0saic fits: a 48-cell grid is pure rectangles with one state per\n  cell.\n- Risks: no one asks for a shareable image. Each person finishes a list\n  once. Munro and Wainwright names are non-ASCII and the lists run to\n  214-282 cells.\n\n### 5. Monthly art-challenge compilation grid (Inktober, Huevember)\n\n- Who: artists doing 31-day prompt challenges. Inktober is running now: the\n  [official 2026 list](https://www.svslearn.com/news/2026/9/1/the-official-inktober-2026-prompt-list)\n  says \"31 drawings in 31 days\".\n- The construct: the prompt list (31 numbered words) plus the artist's daily\n  drawings. Huevember's founder\n  [wants the month compiled](https://xquissive.com/huevember): \"when you put\n  all your artworks next to each other in order, you'd notice a sense of\n  continuity through the colours\".\n- Why m0saic fits: a 31-cell grid of image, day number and prompt word.\n  With no images it renders the host's prompt-list graphic.\n- Risks: there is no data file, and the audience lives in Procreate and\n  Canva. Demand is implied, not shown. Collage apps cover most of it. The\n  defaults would be empty placeholders.\n\n## Pick\n\n**parkrun weekly event-summary card.** It is the only candidate where an\nopened page says a volunteer team makes this exact picture by hand and that\ndoing so is \"quite cumbersome\". The numbers are a fixed set that three\nvolunteer tools already extract every week, which gives the props and their\ndefaults. It also adds a weekly group picture made by event volunteers to a\nshelf of personal PB cards. Bell ringing has the more charming construct,\nbut its evidence is a blank docx certificate, not a recurring post.\n\n## Rejected today\n\n- NetHack ascension card (xlogfile `conduct`/`achieve` bitfields): a lovely\n  rectangle shape, but ascension posts (\"YAAP\") are text plus dumplog links,\n  and nobody I read makes a picture.\n- Concept2 erg piece card: good numbers (split /500m, spm, watts, drag\n  factor), but no opened page asks for a graphic, and ErgData shares a link.\n- Word-count recap card (post-NaNoWriMo): Pacemaker, TrackBear and NaNo 2.0\n  draw the par-line graph, and writers share screenshots of it.\n- CrossFit daily WOD graphic: the workout is a free-text blob, and SugarWOD\n  BoxTV and Canva packs serve it. A PR card had no evidence.\n- parkrun personal milestone card (50/100/250): parkrun already recognises\n  milestones with shirts, it needs names (non-ASCII and privacy risk), and\n  I found no by-hand evidence.\n- osu! replay thumbnails: [osr2png](https://github.com/xjunko/osr2png) is a\n  CLI that already does it.\n- Slay the Spire / Balatro run cards: run trackers serve them, and they need\n  card art.\n- Home coffee roasting (Artisan `.alog`): Artisan prints its own roast\n  report.\n\n## Research record\n\n- Eight parallel sweeps ran about 55 web searches and opened about 35 pages.\n  The per-angle notes, the searches and the failed URLs are in\n  [`scout-notes/`](scout-notes/).\n- I re-opened the pick's sources myself:\n  - ear1grey/parkrun-event-summary\n  - johnsyweb/eventuate\n  - the Eventuate greasyfork page\n  - rwkura/parkrun-milestones\n  - the CCCBR publicity page\n- Failed to open: the Walkhighlands forum and pixiv (403), BellBoard\n  `export.php` (500), metacpan NAOdash (402) and TrackBear's help pages (403).\n  Reddit was not tried (blocked since 2026-09-25).\n","numLines":222,"startLine":1,"totalLines":222}}}
